// NATYAM ERP — Firestore Security Rules
// (Auth, Users, Login Sessions, Students, Admissions, Attendance, Class
// Sessions, Programmes, Certificates, Batches, Staff, Fee Collection,
// Finance, Documents, Admission Drafts, Notifications, Branches,
// Academic Years, Curricula, Curriculum Levels, Holidays & Audit Log)
//
// Two unrelated things are both called "sessions" below — `sessions` is a
// sign-in record (js/data/sessions.repository.firestore.js); `classSessions`
// is a Timetable class occurrence (js/data/classSessions.repository.firestore.js).
// Don't confuse the two collections despite the name.
//
// This is the real security boundary the old IndexedDB app never had.
// Paste this into Firebase Console → Firestore Database → Rules (or deploy
// with `firebase deploy --only firestore:rules` once the Firebase CLI is
// set up) — I have no access to your project to do this myself.
//
// `users` documents are keyed by the person's lowercased email address
// (see js/data/users.repository.firestore.js for why), so `request.auth.
// token.email.lower()` always points straight at "the caller's own
// document" without needing a query.
//
// Role model: Administrator / Owner & Accountant / Teacher & Reception /
// Viewer (Doc 10 §8). Every role-gated collection below mirrors the
// capability grants already defined in js/config/app.config.js's `ROLES`
// table; this file does not re-decide who can do what, only enforces it
// server-side.
//
// 2026-07-30 — Owner role upgrade. `owner_accountant` now holds every
// capability except the system-level ones in that file's
// ADMINISTRATOR_ONLY_CAPABILITIES list, because the academy's owner is also
// its accountant, teacher, registrar and receptionist, and a role that made
// her switch accounts to do her own job described the software rather than
// the business. Concretely, in this file: canAdminister() (below) replaces a
// bare isAdministrator() wherever the gate was standing in for an
// operational capability the Owner now holds, and stays isAdministrator()
// wherever it was standing in for a reserved one. The reserved ones, and
// where each is enforced here:
//
//   data.restore     replaceAll()'s delete-then-recreate on every collection
//                    it touches, plus /settings — restore and erase
//   audit.purge      /auditLog delete
//   role.manage      creating or altering an /users doc whose role is
//                    `administrator` (the escalation the reservation would
//                    otherwise be decoration without)
//   system.configure, system.maintain, security.manage
//                    no collection today — no screen grants them yet
//
// Client-side capability checks and these rules are two expressions of one
// table, and they have to be edited together: a capability granted in
// app.config.js and denied here is a "Missing or insufficient permissions"
// crash, not a locked button.
//
// Milestone P1 added a second, unrelated identity: a Parent/Student Portal
// guardian, who has no /users doc and no role at all — just an authenticated
// caller whose phone/email token claim matches a guardianPhone/guardianEmail
// already on file for one or more active students (isGuardianOfStudent()
// below). This only ever widens `allow read` on students; every write rule
// and every other collection's rules are unchanged.
//
// A per-studentId lookup (isGuardianOfStudentId()) was tried on attendance/
// certificates/invoices/payments too and reverted 2026-07-27: Firestore caps
// get()/exists() calls at 10 for an entire query, not per document, and a
// full-collection read (a backup's safety copy, attendance$.replaceAll(),
// etc.) touches far more than 10 distinct students — it denied the whole
// query with "Missing or insufficient permissions" and broke a live restore.
// The portal's Timetable/Attendance/Programmes/Certificates/Fees pages are
// not yet server-side-authorized beyond students/{id} itself until that's
// redesigned with a denormalized field (see isGuardianOfStudent()'s comment).
//
// 2026-07-27, separately: every repository's replaceAll() (used only by
// restore-from-backup) hard-deletes every existing document in a collection
// before recreating it. Several collections had `allow delete: if false`
// (a deliberate "no hard delete in the normal app UI" policy, unrelated to
// restore) — fine on a first-ever restore with nothing to delete, but it
// silently aborted any later restore the moment that collection already had
// documents, part-way through the fixed write sequence, leaving whatever
// ran before the failure in place and everything after it untouched. Fixed
// by opening `delete` to Administrator alone on admissions/classSessions/
// programs/batches/staff/invoices/payments/branches/academicYears/
// curricula/salaries/expenses/auditLog — the same role already gated
// behind data.restore (then still called backup.manage) to reach restore()
// at all. `update` stays denied
// wherever it already was (auditLog stays append-only otherwise).
//
// Parent Portal Stage 0 added the first two collections in this file that a
// caller with NO Firebase identity at all can touch — `siteContent` (read)
// and `enquiries` (create). Every other collection here, including the
// guardian widening above, still requires request.auth != null at minimum.
// Both are shaped so that being public costs nothing:
//
//   siteContent  Every public-facing section: About Natyam, the Founder,
//                Courses, Branches, Batch Timings, and later Gallery,
//                Events, FAQ, Contact, Testimonials, Website Home and SEO.
//                One document per section. Authored in exactly one place —
//                the Desktop ERP's Settings → Website Content module — and
//                read by natyam-mobile and, later, the Natyam website.
//
//                Note what this collection is NOT: it is not derived from
//                /branches, /batches or the curriculum, and nothing writes
//                to it automatically. The school maintains it by hand, and
//                accepts that a branch address lives in two places, in
//                exchange for one moving part instead of a projection layer.
//                Those operational collections stay closed exactly as they
//                are — which is also why none of this had to open them:
//                rules permit or deny a whole document and cannot hide
//                fields, so publishing /batches would have published
//                capacity, enrolment counts and teacher assignments along
//                with a class time.
//
//   enquiries    A prospective parent's "contact me" record, created before
//                they have any account. Create is open, which is a write
//                surface for the whole internet, so it is the one rule in
//                this file that validates the document field by field: an
//                exact key set, type and length caps on every value, and a
//                forced status/source/createdBy. There is no rate limit at
//                this layer — Firestore rules cannot express one — so spam
//                is a triage problem for Reception, not a data-integrity
//                one. Nothing else in the database is reachable from it.
//
// Everything outside `users`/`meta`/`sessions`/`students`/`admissions`/
// `attendance`/`classSessions`/`programs`/`certificates`/`batches`/`staff`/
// `feePlans`/`invoices`/`payments`/`ledgerEntries`/`expenses`/`salaries`/
// `documents`/`admissionDrafts`/`notifications`/`branches`/`academicYears`/
// `curricula`/`curriculumLevels`/`holidays`/`auditLog`/`siteContent`/
// `parentProfiles`/
// `enquiries` is denied by default. As of Milestone 24 every entity in the
// app is on Firestore — no store is left on IndexedDB with no rules to
// protect it. The final catch-all rule must stay last regardless.

rules_version = '2';
service cloud.firestore {
  match /databases/{database}/documents {

    function signedIn() {
      return request.auth != null;
    }

    function myEmail() {
      return request.auth.token.email.lower();
    }

    function isOwnDoc(userId) {
      return signedIn() && userId == myEmail();
    }

    // Direct path lookup — works because the doc id *is* the caller's email.
    function callerDoc() {
      return get(/databases/$(database)/documents/users/$(myEmail()));
    }

    // A phone-only caller (Milestone P1's Parent/Student Portal — no linked
    // email at all) has no `email` token claim, and myEmail() calling
    // .lower() on a missing claim throws — checked first, same reasoning as
    // exists()-before-get() below: an unguarded check here would poison
    // isProvisionedActiveUser() for every guardian caller.
    function hasEmailClaim() {
      return signedIn() && 'email' in request.auth.token && request.auth.token.email != null;
    }

    // exists() must come first: on a first-ever sign-in the caller has no
    // /users doc yet, and reading .data off get() of a document that
    // doesn't exist throws — which, unguarded, poisons any check built on
    // it and denies even legitimate cases (like bootstrap). && short-
    // circuits, so a missing/inactive/archived doc safely resolves through
    // to "no role" rather than erroring.
    function isProvisionedActiveUser() {
      return signedIn()
        && hasEmailClaim()
        && exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/users/$(myEmail()))
        && callerDoc().data.status == 'active'
        && callerDoc().data.deletedAt == null;
    }

    function myRole() {
      return isProvisionedActiveUser() ? callerDoc().data.role : null;
    }

    function isAdministrator() {
      return myRole() == 'administrator';
    }

    function isTeacherReception() {
      return myRole() == 'teacher_reception';
    }

    function isOwnerAccountant() {
      return myRole() == 'owner_accountant';
    }

    // "Holds every operational capability" — Administrator by way of holding
    // all of them, Owner by way of holding all but the reserved ones. Used
    // wherever a rule needs the full-access set rather than a named
    // capability, which in practice is everywhere the old rules said
    // isAdministrator() for a reason other than the reservations listed in
    // this file's header.
    function canAdminister() {
      return isAdministrator() || isOwnerAccountant();
    }

    // student.edit — Administrator, Owner and Teacher & Reception. Viewer
    // is read-only for Students.
    function canManageStudents() {
      return canAdminister() || isTeacherReception();
    }

    // admission.edit — same role set as canManageStudents() today, but kept
    // as its own named function since admission.edit and student.edit are
    // distinct capabilities in app.config.js that merely happen to share a
    // role set right now; a future change to either grant should not
    // silently affect the other.
    function canManageAdmissions() {
      return canAdminister() || isTeacherReception();
    }

    // admission.approve is granted to Administrator, Owner & Accountant and
    // Teacher & Reception — everyone except Viewer.
    function canApproveAdmissions() {
      return isAdministrator() || isOwnerAccountant() || isTeacherReception();
    }

    function isViewer() {
      return myRole() == 'viewer';
    }

    // attendance.view — all four roles. The Owner gained it in the role
    // upgrade (she teaches classes and takes her own registers), which makes
    // this universal where it used to exclude her.
    function canViewAttendance() {
      return isProvisionedActiveUser();
    }

    // attendance.mark — Administrator, Owner and Teacher & Reception.
    function canMarkAttendance() {
      return canAdminister() || isTeacherReception();
    }

    // True when a write touches only soft-delete bookkeeping fields — used
    // to require the full-access set specifically for archive/restore even
    // where a role otherwise has general edit rights (student.delete is
    // Administrator + Owner; student.edit also includes Teacher & Reception).
    function isArchiveOnly() {
      return request.resource.data.diff(resource.data).affectedKeys()
        .hasOnly(['deletedAt', 'deletedBy', 'updatedAt', 'updatedBy']);
    }

    // Milestone P1 — Parent/Student Portal. A guardian has no /users doc and
    // no role: their identity is just an authenticated caller whose phone or
    // email token claim matches a guardianPhone/guardianEmail already on
    // file for one or more active students (see
    // docs/migrations/PARENT_STUDENT_PORTAL_MILESTONE.md and
    // js/services/portal/guardianAuth.service.js). No new collection, no new
    // role string — this only ever widens `allow read`, never a write rule.
    function callerPhone() {
      return signedIn() ? request.auth.token.phone_number : null;
    }

    function isGuardianOfStudent(studentData) {
      return signedIn() && studentData.status == 'active' && (
        (callerPhone() != null && studentData.guardianPhone == callerPhone()) ||
        (hasEmailClaim() && studentData.guardianEmail == request.auth.token.email)
      );
    }

    // A staff `/users` doc is always keyed by email, even for an account
    // that also signs in via Mobile OTP — a phone-only session token has no
    // email claim at all, so myEmail() can't identify "my own doc" the way
    // it does for Google/Password. One extra get() (a single document, not
    // a query — sessions/{id} is only ever read/written by its own id, never
    // listed, so the 10-get query-wide budget doesn't apply here) confirms
    // the userId being claimed is actually this caller's own account by
    // checking its mobile field against the caller's phone claim. Confirmed
    // 2026-07-28: sessions/{sessionId}'s unconditional myEmail() call was
    // throwing (denying) every Mobile OTP sign-in at session-record
    // creation, for staff and guardians alike, immediately after fixing the
    // audit-log write that was masking it.
    function ownsUserId(userId) {
      return (hasEmailClaim() && userId == myEmail())
        || (callerPhone() != null
            && exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/users/$(userId))
            && get(/databases/$(database)/documents/users/$(userId)).data.mobile == callerPhone());
    }

    // REMOVED (2026-07-27): a per-document get() on a different /students/{id}
    // path for every distinct studentId is not viable on attendance/
    // certificates/invoices/payments — Firestore caps get()/exists() calls at
    // 10 for an ENTIRE query, not per document, and a full-collection read
    // (buildBackup()'s safety copy, attendance$.replaceAll(), etc.) touches
    // far more than 10 distinct students. It silently denied the whole query
    // with "Missing or insufficient permissions" and broke a live restore.
    // Guardian read access to students/{id} itself was unaffected
    // (isGuardianOfStudent() above is a direct field comparison, no extra
    // read, and was never the problem).

    // Milestone P2 (2026-07-28) — the follow-up this comment used to point
    // to: guardianPhone/guardianEmail are now denormalized directly onto
    // attendance/certificates/invoices/payments documents themselves (see
    // each service's create path — attendance.service.js#postRegister(),
    // certificates.service.js#issue(), fees.service.js#createInvoice(), and
    // ledger.repository.firestore.js#postPayment(), which copies its
    // invoice's fields rather than re-reading the student). A direct field
    // comparison here, same shape as isGuardianOfStudent() above, no get().
    //
    // Critical for this to actually work as a QUERY (not just a single-
    // document get): Firestore only authorizes a list request when its own
    // where() filter can prove this condition holds for every possible
    // match. The portal's reads on these four collections were rewritten to
    // query by guardianPhone/guardianEmail directly (each repository's new
    // forGuardian()) instead of by studentId — a studentId-filtered query
    // would still be denied outright no matter what this function checks,
    // the exact failure already hit twice tonight elsewhere (the guardian's
    // own students query, and users$.findByMobile()).
    function isGuardianOfRecord(recordData) {
      return signedIn() && (
        (callerPhone() != null && recordData.guardianPhone == callerPhone()) ||
        (hasEmailClaim() && recordData.guardianEmail == request.auth.token.email)
      );
    }

    match /users/{userId} {
      // A person may always read their own record (to resolve their own
      // role/status on sign-in); an Administrator may read anyone's. The
      // third branch is what makes Mobile OTP resolvable at all:
      // users$.findByMobile() (auth.service.js's resolveProvisionedUser(),
      // the very first read on every Mobile OTP sign-in, staff or
      // guardian) is a QUERY (where('mobile','==',...)), not a get() by
      // this doc's own id — a phone-only caller doesn't know their own
      // email yet, so isOwnDoc(userId) can never match, and isAdministrator()
      // requires an email claim too. Firestore only permits a query when
      // its own where() clause can prove the rule holds for every possible
      // match; since the query filters on the exact same `mobile` field
      // this branch compares, and findByMobile() is only ever called with
      // the caller's own just-verified phone number, this is safe — it can
      // only ever match the one document that is genuinely theirs.
      // Confirmed 2026-07-28: without this, every Mobile OTP sign-in
      // (staff or guardian) failed at this very first lookup, before ever
      // reaching resolveProvisionedUser()'s own status/method checks or
      // the guardian portal fallback.
      allow read: if isOwnDoc(userId) || canAdminister()
        || (callerPhone() != null && resource.data.mobile == callerPhone());

      // Normal provisioning: user.create — Administrator or Owner. An Owner
      // may not mint an Administrator, though: role.manage is reserved, and
      // without this clause every reserved permission would be one
      // self-granted account away (settings.service.js's
      // requireRoleAssignable() is the same check client-side). One-time
      // exception: while `meta/bootstrapped` does not yet exist, the first
      // person to sign in may create themself — as Administrator only. See
      // bootstrapAdministrator() in the repository.
      allow create: if isAdministrator()
        || (isOwnerAccountant() && request.resource.data.role != 'administrator')
        || (isOwnDoc(userId)
            && request.resource.data.role == 'administrator'
            && !exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/meta/bootstrapped));

      // Profile edits, role changes, activate/deactivate/archive (a
      // soft-delete update, not a hard delete) — user.edit, so Administrator
      // or Owner. The Owner may neither touch an existing Administrator's
      // account nor promote anyone into the role: both checks are needed,
      // since either alone leaves the other route open.
      allow update: if isAdministrator()
        || (isOwnerAccountant()
            && resource.data.role != 'administrator'
            && request.resource.data.role != 'administrator');
      allow delete: if false;
    }

    // Set once, atomically, alongside the bootstrap administrator's user
    // doc. Its mere existence retires the bootstrap exception above forever.
    match /meta/bootstrapped {
      allow read: if signedIn();
      allow write: if isAdministrator() || !exists(/databases/$(database)/documents/meta/bootstrapped);
    }

    // Shared business-code counter (USR000001, STU000001, …) — see
    // js/data/sequenceGenerator.firestore.js. Writable by anyone who can
    // create a code-bearing entity; today that's Administrator (users) and
    // Administrator + Teacher & Reception (students), i.e. canManageStudents().
    match /meta/counters {
      allow read, write: if canManageStudents();
    }

    // One document per sign-in (js/data/sessions.repository.firestore.js).
    // A person may create and end only their own; an Administrator may
    // read anyone's, for a future "active sessions" admin view. Never
    // deleted — ended sessions are a soft-delete update (endedAt/reason),
    // the same audit-trail convention every other module uses.
    match /sessions/{sessionId} {
      allow create: if signedIn() && ownsUserId(request.resource.data.userId);
      allow read: if signedIn() && (ownsUserId(resource.data.userId) || isAdministrator());
      allow update: if signedIn() && ownsUserId(resource.data.userId);
      allow delete: if false;
    }

    // Milestone 3 (see ADR-014, docs/migrations/STUDENT_MODULE_MIGRATION.md).
    match /students/{studentId} {
      // Every role has student.view — Administrator, Owner & Accountant,
      // Teacher & Reception, Viewer alike. A guardian may additionally read
      // only their own child's record (Milestone P1).
      allow read: if isProvisionedActiveUser() || isGuardianOfStudent(resource.data);

      allow create: if canManageStudents();

      // A general profile/status edit needs student.edit (Administrator,
      // Owner or Teacher & Reception); a write that *only* touches archive/
      // restore bookkeeping needs student.delete (Administrator or Owner)
      // instead — otherwise a Teacher & Reception account with edit rights
      // could archive a student merely because it can edit one.
      allow update: if (canManageStudents() && !isArchiveOnly())
        || (canAdminister() && isArchiveOnly());

      // Hard delete (students.service.js's deleteStudent(), which also
      // removes the student's invoices/payments/attendance/certificates/
      // documents) requires student.delete — same as archive above.
      allow delete: if canAdminister();
    }

    // Milestone 5 (see ADR-014, docs/migrations/ADMISSIONS_MODULE_MIGRATION.md).
    // Parent Portal Stage 3. A prospective parent — signed in with Google,
    // but with no /users doc and no linked student — submits their own
    // admission application from natyam-mobile. They are not staff, so
    // canManageAdmissions() can never be true for them; this is the only
    // other way into this collection.
    //
    // The four server-forced values are what make it safe. `applicationNo`
    // MUST be null: the official NAT/APP number is allocated by the Desktop
    // ERP at beginReview() from the staff-gated /settings sequence counter,
    // and an applicant must not be able to claim one. `status` must be
    // `submitted`, so nobody can self-approve or self-enrol. `source` marks
    // it as parent-submitted so Reception can tell it from a walk-in. And
    // `submittedByEmail` must equal the caller's own verified token claim,
    // which is what ties the row to them for the read rule below.
    //
    // Deliberately NOT validated here: the full application shape. Unlike
    // /enquiries, this collection is also written by staff with a rich,
    // evolving field set, and pinning an exact key set would break the
    // desktop wizard on its next field addition. The boundary drawn is the
    // one that matters — an applicant cannot forge a number, a status, or
    // somebody else's identity.
    function isSelfSubmittedApplication() {
      let d = request.resource.data;
      return hasEmailClaim()
        && d.source == 'parent_portal'
        && d.submittedByEmail == myEmail()
        && d.status == 'submitted'
        && d.applicationNo == null
        && d.name is string && d.name.size() > 0 && d.name.size() <= 120
        && d.guardianPhone is string && d.guardianPhone.size() >= 8
        && d.guardianPhone.size() <= 20;
    }

    /** An applicant reading back one of their OWN submissions. */
    function isMyOwnApplication() {
      return hasEmailClaim()
        && resource.data.source == 'parent_portal'
        && resource.data.submittedByEmail == myEmail();
    }

    match /admissions/{admissionId} {
      // Every role has admission.view — Administrator, Owner & Accountant,
      // Teacher & Reception, Viewer alike. Widened for Stage 3 so an
      // applicant can track their own application's status, and ONLY their
      // own: the equality on submittedByEmail is the same single-field shape
      // the guardian rules on /students already use, so the matching query in
      // admissions.service.js can be served without exposing the collection.
      allow read: if isProvisionedActiveUser() || isMyOwnApplication();

      // Only submit() requires admission.edit, held by Administrator and
      // Teacher & Reception only — plus a prospective parent submitting their
      // own application, under the constraints above.
      allow create: if canManageAdmissions() || isSelfSubmittedApplication();

      // Every other write (review/approve/reject/reopen/enrol) requires
      // either admission.edit or admission.approve — the union of both is
      // Administrator, Owner & Accountant and Teacher & Reception. This
      // does not replicate the full client-side status-transition state
      // machine field-by-field (a real design task, not attempted here) —
      // it draws the one boundary that matters server-side: Viewer can
      // never write to this collection. See the migration doc's Risks
      // section for this disclosed simplification.
      allow update: if canManageAdmissions() || canApproveAdmissions();

      // No hard-delete path exists in the normal app UI (admissions are
      // only ever soft-deleted) — but admissions$.replaceAll() (restore
      // from a backup) does a real delete-then-recreate on this
      // collection, and 2026-07-27 confirmed that fails outright once the
      // collection already has documents if this stays `false` for
      // everyone. Administrator-only, matching data.restore's own gate.
      allow delete: if isAdministrator();
    }

    // Milestone 6 (see ADR-014). No Leave concept exists in NATYAM; Holiday
    // handling is out of this collection's scope (Dashboard reads the
    // Holidays calendar directly for its own display, unrelated to this
    // rule set). Only Present/Absent are ever written — enforced in the
    // Service layer (attendance.service.js's postRegister()), not
    // duplicated here as a field-value rule.
    match /attendance/{attendanceId} {
      allow read: if canViewAttendance() || isGuardianOfRecord(resource.data);
      allow create, update: if canMarkAttendance();

      // Hard delete (students.service.js's deleteStudent(), which removes
      // a student's attendance history along with their other records)
      // requires student.delete, mirrored here since this collection has no
      // capability of its own for hard deletes.
      allow delete: if canAdminister();
    }

    // Milestone 7 (see ADR-014). Timetable owns Sessions — Attendance only
    // ever references one by id, never writes here (enforced by
    // architecture: session.service.js is the only file that touches this
    // collection). Reusing canManageStudents() for writes deliberately —
    // Session postpone/cancel/schedule actions piggyback on the same
    // student.edit capability batch management already does (a pre-existing
    // convention in this codebase, not something introduced here) rather
    // than inventing a new capability unasked.
    match /classSessions/{sessionId} {
      allow read: if isProvisionedActiveUser();
      allow create, update: if canManageStudents();

      // A Session is never removed via the normal UI, only transitioned to
      // Postponed/Cancelled/Completed — but classSessions$.replaceAll()
      // (restore from a backup) needs a real delete-then-recreate here,
      // same reasoning as admissions above. Administrator-only.
      allow delete: if isAdministrator();
    }

    // program.edit — Administrator, Owner and Teacher & Reception. Kept as
    // its own named function rather than reusing canManageStudents(), even
    // though the role set is identical today, matching the same precedent
    // already used for canManageAdmissions().
    function canManagePrograms() {
      return canAdminister() || isTeacherReception();
    }

    // Milestone 17 (see ADR-014). Programme records support a soft-delete
    // (archive/restore), same convention as Students — a general edit and
    // an archive/restore both need program.edit, so (unlike Students) no
    // field-level split is drawn here; nothing in the application today
    // distinguishes "edit" from "archive" for a programme the way it does
    // for a student.
    match /programs/{programId} {
      // Every role has program.view — Administrator, Owner & Accountant,
      // Teacher & Reception, Viewer alike.
      allow read: if isProvisionedActiveUser();
      allow create, update: if canManagePrograms();

      // No hard-delete path exists in the normal app UI (a programme is
      // only ever soft-deleted) — but programs$.replaceAll() (restore from
      // a backup) needs a real delete-then-recreate here, same reasoning
      // as admissions above. Administrator-only.
      allow delete: if isAdministrator();
    }

    // certificate.issue — Administrator and Owner (Teacher & Reception and
    // Viewer do not hold it). Kept as its own named function rather than
    // reusing canAdminister() inline, matching the canManagePrograms()/
    // canManageAdmissions() precedent.
    function canIssueCertificates() {
      return canAdminister();
    }

    // Milestone 18 (see ADR-014). A certificate is normally revoked, not
    // deleted — update() covers both revoke() and any future edit. The one
    // real hard-delete path is students.service.js's deleteStudent()
    // cascade, which removes a student's certificates along with the rest
    // of their history; that requires student.delete (Administrator only),
    // mirrored here exactly as it already is for Attendance.
    match /certificates/{certificateId} {
      // The Certificates page is gated client-side by program.view, itself
      // granted to every role — Administrator, Owner & Accountant, Teacher
      // & Reception, Viewer alike — so every provisioned user can read here.
      allow read: if isProvisionedActiveUser() || isGuardianOfRecord(resource.data);
      allow create, update: if canIssueCertificates();
      allow delete: if canAdminister();
    }

    // Milestone 19 (see ADR-014). Batch create/edit/close/reopen all piggyback
    // on student.edit in batches.service.js (session.require('student.edit', ...)
    // — there is no separate batch.edit capability) — the same situation
    // classSessions is already in, and this reuses canManageStudents()
    // directly for exactly the same reason that block already documents:
    // no distinct capability exists to justify a dedicated helper. Batches
    // read access is gated client-side by student.view (the Batches/
    // Timetable nav entries), universal across all four roles.
    match /batches/{batchId} {
      allow read: if isProvisionedActiveUser();
      allow create, update: if canManageStudents();

      // No hard-delete path exists in the normal app UI (a batch is only
      // ever closed via update()) — but batches$.replaceAll() (restore
      // from a backup) needs a real delete-then-recreate here; confirmed
      // 2026-07-27 that leaving this `false` for everyone silently aborts
      // a restore the moment the collection already has documents (the
      // exact bug behind students showing "Not placed" after a second
      // restore attempt). Administrator-only, matching data.restore.
      allow delete: if isAdministrator();
    }

    // staff.edit — Administrator and Owner (Teacher & Reception and Viewer
    // hold staff.view only), so hiring, editing, deactivating and
    // reactivating a staff member needs one of those two. Kept as its own
    // named function rather than inlining canAdminister(), matching the
    // precedent already set for canIssueCertificates().
    function canManageStaff() {
      return canAdminister();
    }

    // Milestone 20 (see ADR-014). A staff member is normally deactivated,
    // not deleted — update() covers hire-adjacent edits, deactivate() and
    // reactivate() alike. No hard-delete path exists in the application
    // today — denied outright rather than granted speculatively.
    match /staff/{staffId} {
      // Every role has staff.view — Administrator, Owner & Accountant,
      // Teacher & Reception, Viewer alike.
      allow read: if isProvisionedActiveUser();
      allow create, update: if canManageStaff();

      // No hard-delete path exists in the normal app UI (deactivate/
      // reactivate only) — but staff$.replaceAll() (restore from a
      // backup) needs a real delete-then-recreate here, same reasoning as
      // batches above. Administrator-only.
      allow delete: if isAdministrator();
    }

    // Milestone 21 (see ADR-014). Fee Collection and Finance migrated
    // together — fees.service.js's recordPayment()/refundPayment() and
    // finance.service.js's recordExpense()/updateExpense()/removeExpense()/
    // paySalaries() all wrote across multiple stores (including
    // ledgerEntries) in a single IndexedDB db.unit() transaction; splitting
    // the two modules across separate milestones would have broken that
    // atomicity. The equivalent Firestore transactions now live in
    // js/data/ledger.repository.firestore.js.

    // settings.edit — Administrator and Owner. settings.service.js's
    // createFeePlan()/updateFeePlan()/deleteFeePlan() all require it. Kept
    // as its own named function matching the canManageStaff()/
    // canIssueCertificates() precedent.
    function canManageFeePlans() {
      return canAdminister();
    }

    // fee.collect is granted to Administrator, Owner & Accountant and
    // Teacher & Reception — everyone except Viewer.
    function canCollectFees() {
      return isAdministrator() || isOwnerAccountant() || isTeacherReception();
    }

    // fee.refund is granted to Administrator and Owner & Accountant only —
    // Teacher & Reception can collect but not refund.
    function canRefundFees() {
      return isAdministrator() || isOwnerAccountant();
    }

    // fee.waive is granted to Administrator and Owner & Accountant only,
    // the same role set as fee.refund today — kept as its own named
    // function since the two are distinct capabilities in app.config.js
    // that merely happen to coincide right now (the canManageAdmissions()/
    // canManagePrograms() precedent).
    function canWaiveFees() {
      return isAdministrator() || isOwnerAccountant();
    }

    // finance.view is granted to Administrator, Owner & Accountant and
    // Viewer — Teacher & Reception does not hold it, even though it can
    // create a ledgerEntries document by collecting a fee payment. That
    // asymmetry (can write via canCollectFees(), cannot read here) is a
    // real, pre-existing feature of the app's own capability model, not
    // something this migration introduces.
    function canViewFinance() {
      return isAdministrator() || isOwnerAccountant() || isViewer();
    }

    // finance.edit is granted to Administrator and Owner & Accountant only.
    function canManageFinance() {
      return isAdministrator() || isOwnerAccountant();
    }

    // Fee plan management has always been a hard delete (deleteFeePlan()
    // in settings.service.js), unlike every other module migrated so far —
    // the one place this project allows a genuine delete rather than a
    // soft archive.
    match /feePlans/{feePlanId} {
      allow read: if isProvisionedActiveUser();
      allow create, update, delete: if canManageFeePlans();
    }

    // Read access matches fee.view, held by every role. Write access draws
    // the one boundary that matters server-side — some fee capability is
    // required — rather than replicating the client-side state machine
    // (raise/pay/refund/waive/cancel) field-by-field, the same reasoning
    // already used for Admissions' status transitions.
    match /invoices/{invoiceId} {
      allow read: if isProvisionedActiveUser() || isGuardianOfRecord(resource.data);
      allow create: if canCollectFees();
      allow update: if canCollectFees() || canRefundFees() || canWaiveFees();

      // Two paths need this: deleteStudent()'s cascade (student.delete —
      // Administrator or Owner) and invoices$.replaceAll() (restore from a
      // backup, Administrator alone). The wider of the two governs.
      allow delete: if canAdminister();
    }

    match /payments/{paymentId} {
      allow read: if isProvisionedActiveUser() || isGuardianOfRecord(resource.data);
      allow create: if canCollectFees();
      // Only refundPayment() ever updates an existing payment record.
      allow update: if canRefundFees();

      // deleteStudent()'s cascade removes a student's payments through their
      // invoices (student.delete — Administrator or Owner), and
      // payments$.replaceAll() (restore) needs it too.
      allow delete: if canAdminister();
    }

    // Written by fee collection (a payment/refund posting) as well as
    // Finance itself (an expense, a payroll run, a manual entry or
    // reversal) — read access is Finance-only, matching the asymmetry
    // canViewFinance() documents above. The one real hard-delete path is
    // removeExpense()'s linked-entry deletion.
    match /ledgerEntries/{entryId} {
      allow read: if canViewFinance();
      allow create: if canCollectFees() || canRefundFees() || canManageFinance();
      allow update: if canManageFinance();
      allow delete: if canManageFinance();
    }

    // removeExpense() always soft-deletes (an update, not a Firestore
    // delete) — no hard-delete path exists.
    match /expenses/{expenseId} {
      allow read: if canViewFinance();
      allow create, update: if canManageFinance();

      // removeExpense() always soft-deletes — but expenses$.replaceAll()
      // (restore from a backup) needs a real delete-then-recreate here.
      // Administrator-only.
      allow delete: if isAdministrator();
    }

    match /salaries/{salaryId} {
      allow read: if canViewFinance();
      allow create, update: if canManageFinance();

      // No hard-delete path exists in the normal app UI — but
      // salaries$.replaceAll() (restore from a backup) needs a real
      // delete-then-recreate here. Administrator-only.
      allow delete: if isAdministrator();
    }

    // Milestone 22 (see ADR-014). Documents, Admission Drafts and
    // Notifications — three small, independent modules bundled into one
    // milestone. None has its own capability; each piggybacks on
    // whatever the app already gates it by.

    // No capability check exists on documents$ anywhere except the
    // deleteStudent() cascade (student.delete) — read is only reachable
    // via the /students/:id route (student.view, universal), and no
    // create/update path exists anywhere in the app today.
    match /documents/{documentId} {
      allow read: if isProvisionedActiveUser();
      allow create, update: if false;
      allow delete: if canAdminister();
    }

    // saveDraft()/listDrafts()/loadDraft()/discardDraft() have no
    // session.require of their own — any signed-in user who can see
    // Admissions (all four roles hold admission.view) can already save or
    // discard a draft today. This preserves that exactly rather than
    // tightening it unasked.
    match /admissionDrafts/{draftId} {
      allow read, create, update, delete: if isProvisionedActiveUser();
    }

    // notify()/markRead()/markAllRead()/dismiss()/list()/unreadCount()
    // have no capability check today — the bell badge in js/ui/shell.js
    // fetches unreadCount() for every signed-in session, independent of
    // the /notifications route. The one real check in the app is
    // announce()/removeAnnouncement()'s session.require('settings.edit',
    // ...) — Administrator or Owner — expressed here as a field check on the
    // `announcement` flag those two functions set, a precise translation of
    // that existing check rather than a new restriction.
    match /notifications/{notificationId} {
      allow read, update: if isProvisionedActiveUser();
      allow create: if isProvisionedActiveUser()
        && (!request.resource.data.announcement || canAdminister());
      allow delete: if isProvisionedActiveUser()
        && (!resource.data.announcement || canAdminister());
    }

    // settings.edit — Administrator and Owner. createBranch()/
    // updateBranch()/closeBranch(), createAcademicYear()/setCurrentYear(),
    // and every curriculum/level write in curriculum.service.js all require
    // it. Note the deliberate limit of this capability's scope: it covers the
    // *academy's* configuration, never the system's — Firebase/API/
    // environment settings and role definitions are separate, reserved
    // capabilities (system.configure, role.manage) that no rule here grants.
    function canManageSettings() {
      return canAdminister();
    }

    // Milestone 23 (see ADR-014). Settings reference data — Branches,
    // Academic Years, Curricula, Curriculum Levels and Holidays — the
    // last data-shape migration before the Audit Log. Read broadly
    // (branch/year/curriculum data is used across nearly every screen);
    // write Administrator-only, matching settings.edit's actual grant.

    // branches$.active() is read at every sign-in (js/app.js's
    // hydrateSession()) — this must stay isProvisionedActiveUser(), not
    // anything narrower, or a legitimately signed-in user could be locked
    // out of their own branch list.
    match /branches/{branchId} {
      allow read: if isProvisionedActiveUser();
      allow create, update: if canManageSettings();

      // No hard-delete path exists in the normal app UI (closeBranch() is
      // an update) — but branches$.replaceAll() (restore from a backup)
      // needs a real delete-then-recreate here. Administrator-only.
      allow delete: if isAdministrator();
    }

    match /academicYears/{yearId} {
      allow read: if isProvisionedActiveUser();
      allow create, update: if canManageSettings();

      // No hard-delete path exists in the normal app UI — but
      // academicYears$.replaceAll() (restore from a backup) needs a real
      // delete-then-recreate here. Administrator-only.
      allow delete: if isAdministrator();
    }

    match /curricula/{curriculumId} {
      allow read: if isProvisionedActiveUser();
      allow create, update: if canManageSettings();

      // No hard-delete path exists in the normal app UI — but
      // curricula$.replaceAll() (restore from a backup) needs a real
      // delete-then-recreate here. Administrator-only.
      allow delete: if isAdministrator();
    }

    // No write path exists anywhere in the app today — levels are
    // populated only by a historical schema migration and read
    // thereafter as a dropdown vocabulary. Denied outright rather than
    // granted speculatively, matching the established precedent.
    match /curriculumLevels/{levelId} {
      allow read: if isProvisionedActiveUser();
      allow create, update, delete: if false;
    }

    // No write path exists anywhere in the app today, and the store is
    // never seeded either. Denied outright rather than granted
    // speculatively.
    match /holidays/{holidayId} {
      allow read: if isProvisionedActiveUser();
      allow create, update, delete: if false;
    }

    // The key/value settings store (js/data/settings.repository.firestore.js)
    // — institute details, the document-numbering sequences, lastBackupAt,
    // the structural overrides. It reached Firestore after the 24-entity
    // migration was declared complete and had no rule of any kind here,
    // which the catch-all at the bottom of this file denies outright: with
    // settings on Firestore and this block missing, every role including
    // Administrator fails to read the school's own name.
    //
    // Read is universal among provisioned users (the institute's name and
    // address are on every receipt, certificate and report). Writes need
    // settings.edit, with one deliberate exception: `sequences` is written
    // by nextSequence() whenever anyone raises an admission, invoice,
    // receipt or certificate — a Teacher & Reception account collecting a
    // fee must be able to allocate the next receipt number without holding
    // settings.edit. That is the same create-side asymmetry already accepted
    // for ledgerEntries and auditLog. Deleting a settings document happens
    // only in settings$.replaceAll() (restore) — Administrator alone.
    match /settings/{settingKey} {
      allow read: if isProvisionedActiveUser();
      allow create, update: if canManageSettings()
        || (isProvisionedActiveUser() && settingKey == 'sequences');
      allow delete: if isAdministrator();
    }

    // Milestone 24 (see ADR-014) — the final store off IndexedDB. Every
    // repository's own writeAuditRow() wrapper writes here as a side
    // effect of a normal action across every role, so create is broad —
    // any signed-in active user, not just those with audit.view — the
    // same asymmetry already accepted for ledgerEntries (Milestone 21).
    // Read is audit.view — Administrator and Owner. Delete is the one place
    // the two part company on this collection: audit.purge is reserved, so
    // the Owner can read the whole log and account for anything in it, and
    // can never remove an entry from it. That asymmetry is the point of
    // holding an owner accountable to a record she cannot edit.
    match /auditLog/{entryId} {
      allow read: if canAdminister();
      allow create: if isProvisionedActiveUser();

      // An audit entry is append-only by design — no update path exists,
      // or should ever exist. But audit$.replaceAll() (restore from a
      // backup) needs a real delete (of the old entries) before it
      // recreates them, so delete alone is opened to Administrator —
      // audit.purge and data.restore, both reserved; update stays
      // permanently denied.
      allow update: if false;
      allow delete: if isAdministrator();
    }

    /* ======================================================================
       WEBSITE CONTENT & ENQUIRIES — the public surface.
       ====================================================================== */

    // Every public-facing section: About Natyam, the Founder, Courses,
    // Branches, Batch Timings, and later Gallery, Events, FAQ, Contact,
    // Testimonials, Website Home and SEO. One document per key (js/data/
    // siteContent.repository.firestore.js — the doc id IS the key, same shape
    // as /settings above).
    //
    // Maintained by hand in exactly one place: the Desktop ERP's Settings →
    // Website Content module. Read by natyam-mobile and, later, the Natyam
    // website. Nothing writes here automatically, and this collection is not
    // derived from /branches, /batches or the curriculum — the school
    // maintains public copy separately from operational records by choice,
    // trading a little duplication for an architecture with one moving part.
    //
    // `read: if true` is the whole point of the collection. That is also why
    // none of the operational collections had to be opened: a rule can only
    // allow or deny an ENTIRE document and cannot filter fields, so
    // publishing /branches or /batches would have published every batch's
    // capacity, enrolment count and teacher assignment alongside the address
    // and class time a parent came for. Their rules are untouched.
    //
    // Nothing here is ever a secret: if a value would not go on a public web
    // page, it does not belong in this collection.
    //
    // Writes need settings.edit — Administrator and Owner — the same
    // capability that already governs the institute's own details, since this
    // is the same kind of thing: the school's public description of itself.
    // Delete is Administrator alone, matching every other reference-data
    // collection above.

    // The envelope, enforced lightly. Every document is { value, updatedAt },
    // and `value.kind` is the discriminator that tells any consumer — this
    // app, and the Natyam website later — how to render it without knowing
    // anything else about the key. Pinning those two here means the website
    // can trust the shape rather than defending against it, and a malformed
    // write is rejected at the source instead of surfacing as a blank public
    // page nobody notices.
    //
    // Deliberately NOT stricter than that. The content inside `value` is
    // prose, lists and image URLs that the school edits over time; a rule
    // that enumerated block types would have to be redeployed every time a
    // page gained a paragraph style. Structure is enforced here, content is
    // whitelisted on read by publicContent.service.js's normaliser.
    // `updatedAt` / `updatedBy` / `updatedByName` are system-managed audit
    // metadata, stamped by siteContent.repository.firestore.js's set() and
    // never typed by anyone. They sit at DOCUMENT level, outside `value`,
    // which is what keeps them off every public surface without any
    // filtering: natyam-mobile and the website read `value` and never see
    // them. They are named in the key set here so the write is not rejected;
    // their contents are not constrained, since a caller who could forge them
    // could already write the content itself.
    function isSiteContentEnvelope() {
      let d = request.resource.data;
      return d.keys().hasOnly(['value', 'updatedAt', 'updatedBy', 'updatedByName'])
        && d.value is map
        && d.value.kind in ['page', 'list'];
    }

    match /siteContent/{key} {
      allow read: if true;
      allow create, update: if canManageSettings() && isSiteContentEnvelope();
      allow delete: if isAdministrator();
    }

    // A prospective parent's enquiry, submitted with no account (Stage 2's
    // "Enquiry must be available without login"). Reception follows it up
    // from the Desktop ERP.
    //
    // This validator is the reason `allow create: if true` is safe to write.
    // It pins the document to an EXACT key set — hasOnly() and hasAll() over
    // the same list, so an unexpected field is rejected rather than merely
    // ignored — types and length caps on every value, and server-forced
    // status/source/createdBy that a caller cannot choose. The three
    // staff-only fields must be present and null at create: a submitter
    // cannot arrive pre-triaged, pre-assigned or pre-annotated.
    //
    // What this deliberately does NOT do: prove the phone number is real,
    // prove a human sent it, or limit how many arrive. None of those are
    // expressible in this file. The exposure is bounded instead — a spammer
    // can fill this one collection with junk that Reception deletes, and can
    // reach nothing else in the database through it.
    function isPublicEnquiry() {
      let d = request.resource.data;
      let keys = ['name', 'phone', 'email', 'message', 'branchId',
                  'courseInterest', 'status', 'source', 'createdAt',
                  'createdBy', 'handledBy', 'handledOn', 'note'];
      return d.keys().hasOnly(keys) && d.keys().hasAll(keys)
        // Required, and bounded. The lower bounds are the same ones
        // enquiry.service.js checks client-side; both have to agree or a
        // legitimate submission fails with a permissions error instead of a
        // field message.
        && d.name is string && d.name.size() >= 2 && d.name.size() <= 120
        && d.phone is string && d.phone.size() >= 8 && d.phone.size() <= 20
        && d.createdAt is string && d.createdAt.size() <= 40
        // Server-forced. A new enquiry is always new, always public, and
        // always attributed to no one.
        && d.status == 'new'
        && d.source == 'public'
        && d.createdBy == 'public'
        // Optional, and null when not given — the repository always writes
        // the key, so the shape stays stable for the ERP reading it.
        && (d.email == null || (d.email is string && d.email.size() <= 160))
        && (d.message == null || (d.message is string && d.message.size() <= 1000))
        && (d.branchId == null || (d.branchId is string && d.branchId.size() <= 64))
        && (d.courseInterest == null || (d.courseInterest is string && d.courseInterest.size() <= 120))
        // Reception's own fields. Never the submitter's to set.
        && d.handledBy == null && d.handledOn == null && d.note == null;
    }

    // A parent's own onboarding marker. Created the first time they finish
    // something real — submitting an admission application or an enquiry —
    // and read on every later sign-in to decide whether they still need the
    // Welcome screen.
    //
    // WHAT THIS IS NOT. It is not an identity, not a role, and not a claim to
    // be any student's guardian. Which experience someone gets is decided
    // entirely by the staff/guardian/applicant chain in js/app.js, exactly as
    // before; this document only answers "have they been here before?".
    // Holding one grants no access to anything — a parent with a profile and
    // no linked student still reads no student, fee or attendance record,
    // because those collections' rules are untouched and gate on guardianship,
    // not on this.
    //
    // Keyed by lowercased email, the same convention /users uses, so a caller
    // reaches exactly their own document by path with no query.
    match /parentProfiles/{profileId} {
      // Their own, or any provisioned staff member — Reception answering "has
      // this family been in touch before?" is a reasonable desk question.
      allow read: if isOwnDoc(profileId) || isProvisionedActiveUser();

      // Only ever their own, and only their own email inside it. There is no
      // field here worth forging, but pinning the id to the caller keeps one
      // person from seeding a document under someone else's address.
      //
      // create also accepts isAdministrator(), never update. A restore
      // (js/services/backup.service.js's resetEverything/restore) deletes
      // every document first, so writing a backed-up profile back is always a
      // CREATE in rules terms — never an update, and never anyone's own
      // document, since the admin running the restore is not the parent.
      // Administrator already holds unconditional delete on this exact
      // collection two lines down; this is the same trust extended to the
      // recreate half of the same operation, not a new grant. update stays
      // owner-only — nothing needs an admin editing a parent's profile in
      // place.
      allow create: if (isOwnDoc(profileId) && request.resource.data.email == myEmail())
        || isAdministrator();
      allow update: if isOwnDoc(profileId)
        && request.resource.data.email == myEmail();

      // No delete path exists in the app. A profile is a fact about the past.
      allow delete: if isAdministrator();
    }

    match /enquiries/{enquiryId} {
      // Every role has admission.view, and an enquiry is the stage before an
      // admission — so read matches the /admissions block above exactly.
      allow read: if isProvisionedActiveUser();

      // isPublicEnquiry() alone was the whole rule until a restore needed a
      // second path in: it shape-locks a create to a fresh, untouched
      // enquiry (status 'new', handledBy/handledOn/note null), which is
      // right for the public submitting one and wrong for restoring an
      // enquiry Reception had already triaged. isAdministrator() is not a new
      // grant — delete on this exact collection is already
      // Administrator-only four lines down; this lets the same role recreate
      // what it could already destroy, which a restore needs to do.
      allow create: if isPublicEnquiry() || isAdministrator();

      // Triage — marking one contacted, converted or closed, and recording
      // who dealt with it. Same gate as writing to /admissions, since this
      // is the same desk doing the same job one step earlier. Like that
      // block, this does not replicate a field-by-field status machine
      // server-side; it draws the boundary that matters — a Viewer, and the
      // public, can never write here.
      allow update: if canManageAdmissions() || canApproveAdmissions();

      // Deleting spam is a real operation on this collection, unlike every
      // other one in this file. Administrator alone, matching the delete
      // gate used everywhere else here.
      allow delete: if isAdministrator();
    }

    match /{document=**} {
      allow read, write: if false;
    }
  }
}
